---
title: "Executable content"
description: "Executable cells against a warm kernel: what a language label decides, how to set up Python or R, and how an output becomes a block."
---

This is the heart of "code-block-enhanced HTML." A fenced cell tagged with a
language runs against a warm Jupyter kernel; its outputs become their own blocks
tied to the cell's id. Everything on this page is produced from its own `.tmd`
source.

## Code cells

```{python}
#| echo: true
import numpy as np
np.arange(5).sum()
```

On save, **only cells whose source changed (plus downstream cells) re-execute**,
and the kernel never cold-starts. `{python}` cells run against a warm kernel: point
the server at a Python with `ipykernel` via `TALIESIN_PYTHON`. A cell in any other
language, or with no kernel available, renders as highlighted source rather than
failing. With `TALIESIN_PYTHON` set, Taliesin also pre-warms a small pool of
Python kernels at startup (forked from one preloaded interpreter), so even the
*first* edit of a session is near-instant instead of paying the `import` cost;
without it, Python and R cells just cold-start on demand as before.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Setting up a kernel

Code execution needs a Jupyter kernel. A one-time Python setup:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install ipykernel numpy matplotlib   # + whatever your cells import
export TALIESIN_PYTHON="$(pwd)/.venv/bin/python"
```

Now `preview`/`build` run `{python}` cells against that interpreter. No kernel is
fine too: cells just render as highlighted source, and the preview shows a quiet
"kernel unavailable" note instead of failing.
:::

A cell that takes a while does not make you wait blind. **Output streams into the
preview as the cell produces it**, so a training loop printing an epoch line, or a
progress bar redrawing itself, shows up live beside a running-elapsed badge rather
than appearing all at once at the end. Nothing is required to opt in: print as you
go and you can watch it. Progress bars that redraw with a carriage return (tqdm and
friends) render as one updating line, not one line per frame.

The kernel is resilient: if it crashes mid-session the server detects the dead
process and respawns it on the next run, and a wedged cell is interrupted instead
of locking up the kernel. The cap is on **silence**, not on runtime:
`TALIESIN_CELL_SILENCE` (default 600 seconds) is how long a cell may produce no
output at all before it is sent SIGINT. The budget resets on every line a cell
prints, so a long-running job that reports progress runs to completion however
long it takes, with no configuration. (To bound total runtime regardless of
output, set the optional `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT`.) The dev menu's **Restart
kernel** action drops the kernel,
re-runs every cell against a fresh one, and reloads so `define` values
re-bind.

### `echo`, `include`, and `cache`: three different switches

These three boolean options look similar but control different stages, and it
helps to keep them straight:

| Option | Does the cell run? | Source shown? | Output shown? | Cached in `_freeze/`? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `#\| echo: false` | yes | no | yes | yes |
| `#\| include: false` | yes | no | no | yes |
| `#\| cache: false` | yes | yes | yes | **never** |

`echo: false` hides the source listing only; the cell still runs and its output
still appears. `include: false` goes further and hides *both* source and output,
but the cell **still executes**, so it is the way to run setup that mutates kernel
state (imports, seeding an RNG, defining a helper) without showing anything: a
later cell sees those variables. `cache: false` is orthogonal to both: the cell
runs and shows normally, but its output is never frozen, so it always re-executes
on the next build or preview. Reach for it when a cell reads the clock, hits the
network, or draws from a fresh random source, anything whose value should not be
restored from a stale cache.

::: {.callout-note}
`echo`, `include`, and `cache` also exist as document-wide defaults under the
`execute:` front-matter key, and a per-cell `#|` option overrides the default for
that one cell. See the [cell-options reference](../reference/cell-options.tmd) for
the full table.
:::

### Captions: figures, listings, and tables

`#| fig-cap:` adds a caption *and* makes the output a numbered figure, so a
`#| label: fig-x` cell can be cross-referenced as `@fig-x`. Two siblings caption
the other kinds of output:

- `#| lst-cap:` captions a code *listing* (pair it with a `#| label: lst-x`
  to reference it as `@lst-x`).
- `#| tbl-cap:` captions a *table* the cell prints (pair it with `#| label: tbl-x`
  for `@tbl-x`).

```python
#| label: tbl-summary
#| tbl-cap: "Summary statistics by group"
import pandas as pd
from IPython.display import HTML, display

summary = df.groupby("group").mean().reset_index()
display(HTML(summary.to_html(index=False, border=0)))
```

### Publishing a table, rather than printing one

`#| tbl-cap:` captions whatever the cell **publishes**, and the shortest way to
write a table in either language publishes something you did not mean. Both traps
are quiet: the page builds, the lint is clean, and the defect is visible
only in the rendered output.

In Python, a bare `df` on its own line emits pandas' own repr: a table tagged
`border="1"` and `class="dataframe"`, a row-index column, and a `<style scoped>`
block. `scoped` was removed from the HTML standard and no current browser
implements it, so that style element applies to the whole page instead of to its
own table. `to_html(index=False, border=0)`, as above, emits plain markup that the
page's own table styling reaches. Keep the index when its labels carry meaning (a
`describe()` table, where they name the statistic) and drop it when it is a bare
row number.

In R, `knitr::kable(format = "html")` returns HTML as a *string*. A Jupyter R
kernel prints that string, so the reader gets the markup as text (`<table>` and
all, escaped) in a code block. The same call works under knitr/rmarkdown, which
splices the string in itself, and that is what makes this a trap rather than an
obvious mistake. Publish it as HTML instead:

```r
#| label: tbl-coefs
#| tbl-cap: "Fitted coefficients"
IRdisplay::display_html(as.character(
  kable(tidy(fit), format = "html")
))
```

`corpus/analyst/` uses both idioms, with a comment at each one saying why.

### Folded code

`#| code-fold: true` collapses a listing behind a summary (label it with
`code-summary`), so long setup code stays out of the way until clicked:

```{python}
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "Show the setup"
import numpy as np
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
draws = rng.normal(size=10_000)
round(float(draws.mean()), 3), round(float(draws.std()), 3)
```

### Writing a figure to disk

Inline `{python}` figures are themed for the web (transparent background, neutral
grey axes that read on light **and** dark), but that theming never touches global
`rcParams`, so a plain `fig.savefig("x.pdf")` in a cell already writes a clean,
print-ready figure (black on white):

```{python}
#| label: fig-loss
#| fig-cap: "Loss curve"
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt, numpy as np
fig, ax = plt.subplots()
ax.plot(np.arange(40), np.exp(-0.1 * np.arange(40)))
ax.set_xlabel("iteration"); ax.set_ylabel("loss")
fig.savefig("figures/loss.pdf")
plt.show()
```

Use a vector format (`.pdf`/`.svg`) for line art so it stays sharp at any size;
`.png` at a high `dpi=` for raster content (spectrograms, heatmaps). A cell runs with
its **document's directory** as the working directory, so a relative path lands beside
the source rather than wherever you launched Taliesin — create the folder yourself
(`os.makedirs("figures", exist_ok=True)`). Then in LaTeX:
`\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/loss.pdf}`.

An R cell writes its own file the same way (`ggsave()`).

## Live interactive content

`{js}` cells run client-side via a tiny built-in enhancer (no Observable runtime):
native `viewof` inputs and vendored `Plot` + `d3` all work, and a cell re-runs
when an input it declares changes. A cell tags
itself with `//| viewof: NAME` to publish a control, and a consuming cell lists
`//| input: NAME` to re-run when that control changes (read the current value with
`tali.value("NAME")`).

The reactive surface is small and worth knowing exactly:

- `tali.value("NAME")` reads the live value of an input (a `//| viewof` cell or an
  `{{< input >}}` control) or, failing that, a `define` value of that name.
- `tali.defines.NAME` reads a value published from Python by `define`.
- `//| input: A, B` takes a **comma-separated list** of names; the cell re-runs
  when any one of them (input *or* define) changes. There is also
  `//| name: NAME` to publish this cell's return value under `NAME` for other cells
  to consume. (For the reactive graph itself, see
  [Interactive and explorable documents](interactive.tmd).)

Drag the slider to add odd harmonics to a square-wave Fourier
synthesis and watch the curve sharpen:

```{js}
//| viewof: harmonics
//| echo: false
const wrap = document.createElement("label");
wrap.className = "tali-input";
wrap.append("Harmonics ");
const input = document.createElement("input");
input.type = "range";
input.min = "1"; input.max = "12"; input.step = "1"; input.value = "3";
wrap.append(input);
return wrap;
```

```{js}
//| input: harmonics
//| echo: false
const harmonics = tali.value("harmonics");
return Plot.lineY(
  Array.from({length: 500}, (_, t) =>
    d3.range(1, harmonics + 1).reduce(
      (sum, k) => sum + Math.sin(2 * Math.PI * (2 * k - 1) * t / 500) / (2 * k - 1), 0)),
  {curve: "basis", stroke: "#4c8dff"}
).plot({height: 200, marginLeft: 36, y: {label: "amplitude"}});
```


### The Python → JS bridge

`define()` in a Python cell hands values to `{js}` cells, so you can **compute
in Python and render reactively in the browser**. Its signature is keyword-only,
`define(name=value, ...)`: each keyword becomes a published name, and each
value is JSON-serialized into the page for the `{js}` runtime to pick up. A `{js}`
cell then reads the value as `tali.defines.NAME` (or `tali.value("NAME")`, which
falls back to defines when no live input owns that name). In live preview the
Python cell executes *after* the page loads, so its values arrive a moment after
the `{js}` cells; a `{js}` cell reads them and re-runs automatically when they land
(and rebinds when a Python input changes), so the cell recomputes in place without
a reload. Guard against the value not having arrived yet with an early `return`.

```{python}
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "Python: sample a noisy sine wave"
import math, random
random.seed(7)
signal = [math.sin(2 * math.pi * i / 40) + random.uniform(-0.3, 0.3) for i in range(120)]
define(signal=signal)
```

```{js}
//| echo: false
const signal = tali.defines.signal;
if (!signal) return;
return Plot.plot({
  height: 180, marginLeft: 36,
  marks: [Plot.ruleY([0]), Plot.lineY(signal, {stroke: "#2bb673"})]
});
```

### 3D graphics (Three.js / WebGL)

Because a `{js}` cell can `await import()` any ES module, **3D visualizations work
too**. This cell pulls Three.js and `OrbitControls` from a CDN, wraps an equirectangular world
map onto a sphere, and returns the live `<canvas>`. Drag to spin the globe (it
also auto-rotates), the **⛶** button takes it fullscreen, and the renderer is
transparent so the globe follows the light/dark theme.

Every cell is handed an `invalidation` promise. It **resolves the moment the
block is about to re-render or be unmounted** (an edit changes the block's id and
the client replaces the node, or an upstream input fires a re-run). Hang your
teardown off `invalidation.then(...)` to cancel anything long-lived: an animation
loop, an event listener, a WebGL context. The Three.js cell below relies on it,
calling `cancelAnimationFrame` and disposing the renderer when the promise
resolves, so a live save never leaks a running loop or a stranded GL context.

```{js}
//| echo: false
const THREE = await import("https://esm.sh/three@0.163.0");
const { OrbitControls } = await import(
  "https://esm.sh/three@0.163.0/examples/jsm/controls/OrbitControls"
);

const w = 600, h = 360;
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true, alpha: true });
renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2));
renderer.setSize(w, h, false);
renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);
const cv = renderer.domElement;
cv.style.cssText = `display:block;width:${w}px;max-width:100%;height:auto`;

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(40, w / h, 0.1, 100);
camera.position.set(0, 0, 4);
scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 1.7));
const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 0.6);
key.position.set(5, 3, 5);
scene.add(key);

const globe = new THREE.Mesh(
  new THREE.SphereGeometry(1.3, 64, 64),
  new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ roughness: 1, metalness: 0 })
);
scene.add(globe);

function graticule() {
  const c = document.createElement("canvas");
  c.width = 1024; c.height = 512;
  const x = c.getContext("2d");
  x.fillStyle = "#1f3a5f"; x.fillRect(0, 0, c.width, c.height);
  x.strokeStyle = "rgba(255,255,255,0.35)"; x.lineWidth = 2;
  for (let lon = 0; lon <= c.width; lon += c.width / 24) {
    x.beginPath(); x.moveTo(lon, 0); x.lineTo(lon, c.height); x.stroke();
  }
  for (let lat = 0; lat <= c.height; lat += c.height / 12) {
    x.beginPath(); x.moveTo(0, lat); x.lineTo(c.width, lat); x.stroke();
  }
  return new THREE.CanvasTexture(c);
}
new THREE.TextureLoader().load(
  "https://unpkg.com/three-globe/example/img/earth-day.jpg",
  (tex) => {
    tex.colorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
    globe.material.map = tex;
    globe.material.emissiveMap = tex;
    globe.material.emissive = new THREE.Color(0xffffff);
    globe.material.emissiveIntensity = 0.3;
    globe.material.needsUpdate = true;
  },
  undefined,
  () => { globe.material.map = graticule(); globe.material.needsUpdate = true; }
);

const ctrl = new OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);
ctrl.enableDamping = true;
ctrl.dampingFactor = 0.06;
ctrl.enablePan = false;
ctrl.minDistance = 2;
ctrl.maxDistance = 8;
ctrl.autoRotate = true;
ctrl.autoRotateSpeed = 0.6;

let raf;
(function animate() {
  raf = requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  ctrl.update();
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
})();

const box = document.createElement("div");
box.style.cssText = "position:relative;display:inline-block;max-width:100%";
box.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

const fsBtn = document.createElement("button");
fsBtn.textContent = "⛶ Fullscreen";
fsBtn.style.cssText =
  "position:absolute;bottom:8px;right:8px;padding:4px 10px;font-size:12px;cursor:pointer;" +
  "background:rgba(30,30,30,.75);color:#ddd;border:1px solid #555;border-radius:4px;backdrop-filter:blur(4px)";
fsBtn.onclick = () =>
  document.fullscreenElement ? document.exitFullscreen() : box.requestFullscreen();
box.appendChild(fsBtn);

function onFS() {
  const fs = document.fullscreenElement === box;
  if (fs) {
    box.style.maxWidth = "none"; box.style.width = "100vw"; box.style.height = "100vh";
    cv.style.cssText = "display:block;width:100%;height:100%";
    const fw = box.clientWidth, fh = box.clientHeight;
    renderer.setSize(fw, fh, false);
    camera.aspect = fw / fh;
  } else {
    box.style.maxWidth = "100%"; box.style.width = ""; box.style.height = "";
    renderer.setSize(w, h, false);
    camera.aspect = w / h;
    cv.style.cssText = `display:block;width:${w}px;max-width:100%;height:auto`;
  }
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  const cs = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement);
  box.style.background = fs
    ? (cs.getPropertyValue("--tali-bg").trim() || getComputedStyle(document.body).backgroundColor)
    : "";
  fsBtn.textContent = fs ? "✕ Exit fullscreen" : "⛶ Fullscreen";
}
document.addEventListener("fullscreenchange", onFS);

invalidation.then(() => {
  cancelAnimationFrame(raf); ctrl.dispose(); renderer.dispose();
  document.removeEventListener("fullscreenchange", onFS);
});

return box;
```
